@startuml 05-wire-codecs
!include _theme.iuml

title The two wire codecs — msgpack is what Terraform speaks, JSON is what it stores and shows

skinparam nodesep 14
skinparam ranksep 26

rectangle "**a CtyValue, and the CtyType to write it against**\nBoth codecs are type-directed in both directions: the wire cannot tell\na list from a set from a tuple, so the type is supplied, never guessed." as SRC <<core>>

  rectangle "**cty_to_msgpack(value, schema)**" as MP_ENC <<plumbing>>
  rectangle "deep mark check, once, up front\ncollect_marks_deep -> **CtyMarksSerializationError**   //[a]//" as MP_MARKS <<capsule>>
  rectangle "**_convert_value_to_serializable(value, schema, path)**\nshallow mark check at every level, for the path\n**dynamic -> an envelope, first** //[e]// · unknown -> a marker · null -> None\nobject / map -> dict, keys sorted\nlist -> array · **set -> sorted by set_order.order_key** //[l]//\ntuple -> array, positionally · Decimal -> //[b]//" as MP_CONV <<plumbing>>
  rectangle "**msgpack.packb(..., use_bin_type=True)**" as MP_PACK <<plumbing>>

  rectangle "**cty_from_msgpack(data, cty_type)**\nempty input -> DeserializationError" as MP_DEC <<plumbing>>
  rectangle "**msgpack.unpackb(ext_hook=_ext_hook,**\n**raw=False, strict_map_key=False)**" as MP_UNPACK <<plumbing>>
  rectangle "**_unpacked_to_cty(data, schema)**\nan UnknownValue marker -> CtyValue.unknown(schema, marker)\nNone -> CtyValue.null(schema)\nanything else -> schema.validate(data)" as MP_REBUILD <<plumbing>>

  rectangle "**unrefined unknown → ExtType(0, b'\\x00')**\nthe three bytes **d4 00 00**   //[c]//" as UNK0 <<dynamic>>
  rectangle "**refined unknown → ExtType(12, packb(payload))**\ninteger-keyed, only the keys that are set:\n**1** is_known_null · **2** string_prefix\n**3**/**4** number bounds · **5**/**6** length bounds   //[d]//" as UNK12 <<dynamic>>
  rectangle "**the dynamic envelope → [type-spec JSON as bytes, value]**\nout: encode_cty_type_to_wire_json, dumped tight, UTF-8\nback: parse_tf_type_to_ctytype, then decode against it   //[e]//" as DYNENV <<structural>>
  rectangle "**_ext_hook(code, data)**\n**0** -> UNREFINED_UNKNOWN\n**12** -> _decode_refined_unknown_payload(data)\n**any other code** -> UNREFINED_UNKNOWN, per protocol" as EXTHOOK <<dynamic>>

  rectangle "**cty_to_json(value, cty_type)**\ndeep mark check, then convert() if not conformant   //[f]//" as JS_ENC <<plumbing>>
  rectangle "**_marshal(value, cty_type, path)**\n**an unknown is an error here, not a null**   //[g]//\nstrings: ensure_ascii=False + Go's HTML escapes   //[h]//\nnumbers: infinity refused; plain decimal only\nsets: the **same** hash-byte order msgpack uses //[l]//\nobjects: attributes sorted, only those present\ncapsules: a Bytes buffer as base64, any other refused\ndynamic: an object of value + type" as JS_MAR <<plumbing>>

  rectangle "**cty_from_json(payload, cty_type)**\nparse_float / parse_int = **_RawNumber**   //[i]//" as JS_DEC <<plumbing>>
  rectangle "**_unmarshal(raw, cty_type, path)**\nprimitives coerce through go-cty's small explicit table   //[j]//\nobject: an undeclared attribute is an **error**,\nan omitted one becomes **null**\ndynamic: an object carrying both value and type" as JS_UNM <<plumbing>>
  rectangle "**implied_json_type(payload)**\nan array implies a **tuple**, never a list   //[k]//" as JS_IMP <<structural>>

rectangle "**bytes on the wire**\ntfplugin6.DynamicValue has a msgpack field and a json field.\nThere is no third field, which is why marks cannot travel here." as WIRE <<external>>

UNK0 -[hidden]down- UNK12
SRC --> MP_ENC
SRC --> JS_ENC
MP_ENC --> MP_MARKS
MP_MARKS --> MP_CONV
MP_CONV --> UNK0
MP_CONV --> UNK12
MP_CONV --> DYNENV
MP_CONV --> MP_PACK
MP_PACK --> WIRE
WIRE --> MP_DEC
MP_DEC --> MP_UNPACK
MP_UNPACK --> EXTHOOK
EXTHOOK --> MP_REBUILD
MP_UNPACK --> MP_REBUILD
MP_UNPACK --> DYNENV
JS_ENC --> JS_MAR
JS_MAR --> WIRE
WIRE --> JS_DEC
JS_DEC --> JS_UNM
JS_UNM --> JS_IMP

legend bottom
  |= |= the detail behind each step |
  | **[a]** | Asked deeply here //and// shallowly at every level below. The per-level check is what supplies the path to the\noffending value, but it only sees what the encoder descends into — and it never descends into an unknown,\nwhich a container becomes as soon as any element is unknown. Without the deep pre-check, a marked unknown\ninside a list serialized silently, dropping exactly the flag the refusal exists to protect. |
  | **[b]** | An integer inside int64 becomes a msgpack integer; outside it, the decimal text. A non-integer becomes a\nfloat64 **only when the conversion is exact**, tested as Decimal(float(d)) == d. Testing against str(float(d))\ninstead answered "nothing lost" for 0.1 — whose true binary value is 0.1000000000000000055… — so this wrote a\nfloat where go-cty writes the string "0.1". A perpetual diff on every non-integer attribute.\n**A NaN is refused here**: go-cty's number is a big.Float, which has none, so the text "NaN" this used to\nwrite comes back from go-cty as "number is required". ±Inf is fine and round-trips as a float64. |
  | **[c]** | One data byte whose //value// is irrelevant, because one byte is what makes the encoding a msgpack fixext1.\nAn empty payload is legal msgpack and decodes to the same value, but it is c7 00 00 — a different\nserialization of the same unknown. Terraform compares serialized state, so the bytes have to match too. |
  | **[d]** | string_prefix is capped at 256 bytes and then re-trimmed by safe_known_prefix, so the cut cannot land\nmid-grapheme-cluster. Each number bound is written as [number, inclusive] through the ordinary number\nserializer, matching go-cty byte-for-byte — raw UTF-8 digit bytes would decode to the same value but not\nthe same bytes, and Terraform compares serialized state. An empty payload falls back to the ext-0 form. |
  | **[e]** | A dynamic slot carries no type of its own, so the concrete type has to travel beside the value or the far\nside has nothing to decode against. **This holds for an unknown and a null too**, which is why the dynamic\nbranch is taken before either: checking knownness first sent unknown-of-string as a bare d4 00 00 and\nnull-of-string as a bare c0, and go-cty reads both of those back as //dynamic// rather than //string//. |
  | **[f]** | Converting is go-cty's documented behaviour for a non-conformant value. The mark check runs **before** the\nconversion, because conversion rebuilds a value and a rebuilt value that quietly lost a mark would then\nserialize without complaint. |
  | **[g]** | There is no JSON spelling for "not yet decided", and writing null would turn a value that has not been\ncomputed into one that definitively has no value. |
  | **[h]** | Go's encoding/json escapes < > & U+2028 U+2029 by default and Python's json does not. Those bytes end up in\nstate files that are compared textually, so a < written two ways is a diff on every plan. Non-ASCII is //not//\nin the table: Go writes it raw. |
  | **[i]** | A Decimal that remembers the digits it was written with. Unmarshalling a JSON number into a string yields\n//the token//, so 1.50 stays "1.50" and 1e2 stays "1e2" — neither is recoverable from the parsed number. |
  | **[j]** | string accepts a string, a number's literal text, or a bool; number accepts a number or a string holding\none; bool accepts a bool or a string holding one. Every other combination is refused. Refusing the lot\ninstead rejects state and plan JSON that go-cty reads without complaint. |
  | **[k]** | JSON gives no guarantee that an array's elements share a type, and a list would have to invent one. |
  | **[l]** | go-cty orders a set of //primitive// elements by value and every other element type by the bytes of
makeSetHashBytes, which is a different comparison — a set of tuples writes [12] before [1]. That order
reaches the wire, so both codecs use it. Identity stays with _canonical_sort_key, since the hash renders a
number at ten significant digits. |
endlegend

@enduml
